1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,064
Here is a small app on one virtual machine:

2
00:00:03,064 --> 00:00:07,125
a web server in front,
a frontend and two APIs behind it.

3
00:00:07,445 --> 00:00:10,453
If one of them crashes in the night,
who restarts it?

4
00:00:10,700 --> 00:00:13,943
At two in the morning,
one API container crashes.

5
00:00:14,263 --> 00:00:17,327
The app owner finds out when users complain,

6
00:00:17,327 --> 00:00:21,367
and the operator logs in over SSH
and restarts it by hand.

7
00:00:21,617 --> 00:00:25,226
In lecture 20,
Docker could restart a crashed container,

8
00:00:25,226 --> 00:00:27,095
but only on that one machine:

9
00:00:27,095 --> 00:00:30,961
if the machine itself failed,
the whole site waited for you.

10
00:00:31,200 --> 00:00:33,717
So the app owner asks for several servers.

11
00:00:34,037 --> 00:00:39,051
The operator answers with three machines,
and a backup copy beside each API.

12
00:00:39,300 --> 00:00:42,351
Each backup sits
on the same machine as its original.

13
00:00:42,671 --> 00:00:45,767
Name one failure
that still takes that API down,

14
00:00:45,767 --> 00:00:48,025
and say who switches to the backup.

15
00:00:56,867 --> 00:00:58,128
The machine itself:

16
00:00:58,128 --> 00:01:01,646
one failed disk or network
takes both copies at once.

17
00:01:01,966 --> 00:01:06,509
And even when a backup survives,
a person still has to switch over by hand.

18
00:01:06,750 --> 00:01:09,822
The lecture wants three jobs done
with nobody awake.

19
00:01:10,142 --> 00:01:12,745
Failover: a working copy takes over.

20
00:01:13,065 --> 00:01:16,094
Load balancing:
requests spread across copies.

21
00:01:16,414 --> 00:01:18,953
Scaling: more copies when traffic grows.

22
00:01:19,200 --> 00:01:21,522
This card comes back each time we see

23
00:01:21,522 --> 00:01:24,597
what does one of these jobs,
and we tick it then.

24
00:01:24,917 --> 00:01:27,221
The tool for all three is Kubernetes.

25
00:01:29,267 --> 00:01:31,246
Kubernetes is an orchestrator:

26
00:01:31,246 --> 00:01:34,346
it runs containers
across a cluster of machines

27
00:01:34,346 --> 00:01:36,691
and keeps them the way you declared.

28
00:01:37,011 --> 00:01:41,747
The name is Greek for helmsman,
and Helm, a tool in part 2, borrows it.

29
00:01:41,984 --> 00:01:46,144
On the left, one machine runs
every container on one Docker engine.

30
00:01:46,464 --> 00:01:50,599
On the right, many machines,
called nodes, share the containers,

31
00:01:50,599 --> 00:01:53,120
and one control plane coordinates them.

32
00:01:53,367 --> 00:01:57,451
A word of warning:
here, deployment means a way of deploying,

33
00:01:57,451 --> 00:02:00,130
not the Deployment object we meet later.

34
00:02:00,367 --> 00:02:03,610
In lecture 20 the cheese app ran
on one machine.

35
00:02:03,930 --> 00:02:08,111
Now the same app goes to a cluster,
and the tools gain kubectl.

36
00:02:08,431 --> 00:02:12,660
Our toolbox already has it,
plus Helm and the GKE auth plugin,

37
00:02:12,660 --> 00:02:14,639
which the slides do not list.

38
00:02:14,884 --> 00:02:16,010
To follow along:

39
00:02:16,010 --> 00:02:20,021
cheese-app-v3 with your secrets folder
beside it, Docker,

40
00:02:20,021 --> 00:02:22,799
and a Google Cloud project with billing.

41
00:02:23,119 --> 00:02:27,449
A cluster costs money by the hour,
so each part ends by deleting it.

42
00:02:27,684 --> 00:02:32,299
The tracker names the README step,
code panels name their file and lines,

43
00:02:32,299 --> 00:02:34,639
and your turn means pause and run it.

44
00:02:34,959 --> 00:02:37,796
A grey tag in the corner
names the lecture slides.

45
00:02:38,034 --> 00:02:41,853
One honest note:
our recording machine has no Google account.

46
00:02:42,173 --> 00:02:44,739
What runs on a machine you own is real;

47
00:02:44,739 --> 00:02:48,029
every Google step stops
at its first honest error,

48
00:02:48,029 --> 00:02:49,938
and we say what yours prints.

49
00:02:50,184 --> 00:02:53,976
We follow main,
the branch the course site links,

50
00:02:53,976 --> 00:02:56,221
at this November 2025 commit.

51
00:02:56,541 --> 00:02:58,482
Git log tells you which one you have.

52
00:02:58,717 --> 00:03:01,512
Part 1 does the lecture's warm-up by hand.

53
00:03:01,832 --> 00:03:04,264
Part 2 deploys the cheese app as code.

54
00:03:05,400 --> 00:03:06,893
Let's open the cluster.

55
00:03:07,213 --> 00:03:09,368
The lecture draws it in three steps.

56
00:03:09,688 --> 00:03:13,823
First: one control plane,
with arrows to several worker nodes,

57
00:03:13,823 --> 00:03:16,024
each an ordinary virtual machine.

58
00:03:16,267 --> 00:03:18,294
Second, the two planes get names.

59
00:03:18,614 --> 00:03:20,406
The control plane is the brain.

60
00:03:20,726 --> 00:03:24,544
On GKE, Google runs it,
and you never see its machines.

61
00:03:24,864 --> 00:03:26,959
The workers carry your containers:

62
00:03:26,959 --> 00:03:29,856
for the cheese app,
a pool of one or two nodes.

63
00:03:30,100 --> 00:03:31,295
Third, the parts.

64
00:03:31,615 --> 00:03:36,841
The API server is the single entry point;
every other part talks only to it.

65
00:03:37,161 --> 00:03:41,983
Etcd is the memory of the cluster:
its state and configuration.

66
00:03:42,217 --> 00:03:44,734
The scheduler picks a node for each new Pod.

67
00:03:45,054 --> 00:03:46,803
The controllers keep watching

68
00:03:46,803 --> 00:03:50,662
and fix any difference
between what you asked for and what runs;

69
00:03:50,662 --> 00:03:53,737
some of them ask the cloud
for nodes and addresses.

70
00:03:53,983 --> 00:03:58,079
On every worker,
the kubelet is the hands of the API server:

71
00:03:58,079 --> 00:04:00,468
it starts and stops the containers.

72
00:04:00,788 --> 00:04:03,451
kube-proxy routes traffic for Services,

73
00:04:03,451 --> 00:04:07,615
and a container runtime runs the containers,
grouped in Pods.

74
00:04:07,850 --> 00:04:11,327
A small note:
the slide names Docker as the runtime.

75
00:04:11,647 --> 00:04:16,895
On GKE it is containerd,
line 52 of create_cluster.py.

76
00:04:17,215 --> 00:04:20,821
You still build images with Docker,
and they run unchanged.

77
00:04:21,067 --> 00:04:22,560
Now follow the arrows.

78
00:04:22,880 --> 00:04:25,793
Every kubectl command goes to one place,

79
00:04:25,793 --> 00:04:29,216
the API server,
which stores your wish in etcd.

80
00:04:29,536 --> 00:04:32,480
The scheduler and the kubelets
pick up their work from it.

81
00:04:32,717 --> 00:04:35,540
The lecture gives kubectl one grammar:

82
00:04:35,540 --> 00:04:39,032
a command,
a type, an optional name, and flags.

83
00:04:39,352 --> 00:04:40,376
Get nodes.

84
00:04:40,696 --> 00:04:41,696
Get pods.

85
00:04:41,997 --> 00:04:44,727
Get pods, with a flag for every namespace.

86
00:04:44,967 --> 00:04:48,423
When something is stuck,
ask which of these parts is waiting.

87
00:04:48,743 --> 00:04:51,454
Four lamps on this picture
will light up later,

88
00:04:51,454 --> 00:04:53,415
each with a symptom you will meet.

89
00:04:54,550 --> 00:04:58,198
The README keeps this warm-up
after the cheese app's deployment.

90
00:04:58,518 --> 00:05:01,296
The lecture does it first, and so do we:

91
00:05:01,296 --> 00:05:04,491
it is the gentlest first contact
with kubectl.

92
00:05:04,733 --> 00:05:07,741
First, enable five APIs in the console.

93
00:05:08,061 --> 00:05:11,901
A small slip: Google Container Registry
has been shut down.

94
00:05:12,221 --> 00:05:14,589
Enable Artifact Registry instead.

95
00:05:14,833 --> 00:05:18,289
Then start the toolbox: from the src folder,

96
00:05:18,289 --> 00:05:21,980
change into deployment and run
docker-shell.sh.

97
00:05:22,300 --> 00:05:24,024
As committed, it fails:

98
00:05:24,024 --> 00:05:27,847
404 Not Found from Debian,
then pull access denied.

99
00:05:28,083 --> 00:05:30,869
If you did lecture 20, you have seen this:

100
00:05:30,869 --> 00:05:35,379
the base image is Debian bullseye,
whose security packages are gone.

101
00:05:35,699 --> 00:05:38,686
On Windows there is no docker-shell.bat.

102
00:05:39,006 --> 00:05:40,393
Use WSL.

103
00:05:40,633 --> 00:05:44,238
The fix is the same two one-line edits,
an extra here.

104
00:05:44,558 --> 00:05:46,567
Bullseye becomes bookworm,

105
00:05:46,567 --> 00:05:50,173
and google-cloud-sdk becomes
google-cloud-cli,

106
00:05:50,173 --> 00:05:52,132
with its GKE auth plugin.

107
00:05:52,452 --> 00:05:56,143
Every kubectl call to GKE goes
through that plugin.

108
00:05:56,383 --> 00:06:02,335
As in lecture 20, also put your project
on line 13 of docker-shell.sh.

109
00:06:02,583 --> 00:06:06,423
Run it again:
it builds in about 92 seconds and starts.

110
00:06:06,743 --> 00:06:10,823
The login then fails,
because our key belongs to no account;

111
00:06:10,823 --> 00:06:14,359
with yours,
these lines say activated and logged in.

112
00:06:14,600 --> 00:06:19,187
Pause here and start your toolbox;
the rest of this part runs at its prompt.

113
00:06:19,433 --> 00:06:20,926
Next, check the tools.

114
00:06:21,246 --> 00:06:24,148
The gcloud tool
and its auth plugin are there.

115
00:06:24,468 --> 00:06:28,781
Plain kubectl version prints the client,
then connection refused:

116
00:06:28,781 --> 00:06:32,895
there is no cluster yet,
so the server half has nobody to ask.

117
00:06:33,883 --> 00:06:38,059
A small surprise:
the Dockerfile pins one kubectl version,

118
00:06:38,059 --> 00:06:43,099
but Google's own package wins:
a dispatcher that matches each cluster.

119
00:06:43,333 --> 00:06:47,152
Last, check that you are authenticated:
gcloud auth list.

120
00:06:47,472 --> 00:06:49,737
Ours says No credentialed accounts;

121
00:06:49,737 --> 00:06:53,168
yours lists the deployment
service account as active.

122
00:06:54,417 --> 00:06:57,821
Now Create Cluster:
gcloud container clusters

123
00:06:57,821 --> 00:07:01,756
create test-cluster
--num-nodes 2 --zone us-east1-c.

124
00:07:02,076 --> 00:07:04,892
Ours stops at once: no active account.

125
00:07:05,212 --> 00:07:09,073
Pause and run yours;
it costs money every hour it exists.

126
00:07:09,717 --> 00:07:12,537
Yours takes a short wait, prints a table,

127
00:07:12,537 --> 00:07:17,077
and adds an entry to your kubeconfig
so kubectl knows where to go.

128
00:07:17,397 --> 00:07:19,015
The slide makes one node;

129
00:07:19,015 --> 00:07:22,965
the README two, so two copies
can land on different machines.

130
00:07:23,200 --> 00:07:28,683
To keep going, we start a small real cluster
on the laptop with a tool called kind:

131
00:07:28,683 --> 00:07:30,731
one control plane, two workers.

132
00:07:31,051 --> 00:07:35,445
Same API server,
same kubectl, same YAML, but no Google.

133
00:07:35,765 --> 00:07:38,176
It is not in the README; don't run it.

134
00:07:38,750 --> 00:07:42,620
Here even the control plane
is a container we can see;

135
00:07:42,620 --> 00:07:44,340
on GKE, Google hides it.

136
00:07:44,584 --> 00:07:46,625
Next, look around the console:

137
00:07:46,625 --> 00:07:51,389
Kubernetes Engine, your cluster,
its nodes, and the Pods on each node.

138
00:07:51,709 --> 00:07:55,613
In Compute Engine,
your nodes are ordinary virtual machines.

139
00:07:56,350 --> 00:07:58,505
Then try some kubectl commands.

140
00:07:58,825 --> 00:08:00,999
First an extra, kubectl version:

141
00:08:00,999 --> 00:08:05,417
the dispatcher picked a client
that matches the server's version.

142
00:08:05,967 --> 00:08:07,796
The first README command,

143
00:08:07,796 --> 00:08:11,236
get all,
shows only a service named kubernetes,

144
00:08:11,236 --> 00:08:13,690
the door to the API server itself.

145
00:08:14,010 --> 00:08:16,592
We are looking
in the namespace called default;

146
00:08:16,592 --> 00:08:17,636
more on that later.

147
00:08:18,184 --> 00:08:21,113
With all namespaces, kube-system appears,

148
00:08:21,113 --> 00:08:24,542
and here you can spot
the control plane by name:

149
00:08:24,542 --> 00:08:28,829
etcd, the API server,
the controller manager, the scheduler.

150
00:08:29,149 --> 00:08:31,026
On GKE those stay hidden.

151
00:08:31,267 --> 00:08:34,385
componentstatuses prints
a deprecation warning,

152
00:08:34,385 --> 00:08:37,304
which is expected, then three healthy lines.

153
00:08:37,624 --> 00:08:41,103
Get nodes is the better check:
three nodes ready here;

154
00:08:41,103 --> 00:08:44,003
yours shows two workers
and no control plane.

155
00:08:44,323 --> 00:08:45,966
Pause and try all five.

156
00:08:47,200 --> 00:08:52,320
The warm-up deploys a small game,
tic-tac-toe, from one file with two objects.

157
00:08:52,640 --> 00:08:57,568
The Deployment says what runs in a Pod,
how many copies, and to keep them running.

158
00:08:57,888 --> 00:09:01,536
The Service says how traffic reaches them,
and lets Pods die.

159
00:09:01,783 --> 00:09:04,556
The slide trims the lines
that glue them together.

160
00:09:04,876 --> 00:09:08,089
The template gives every Pod a label, run:
web;

161
00:09:08,089 --> 00:09:11,917
both objects find their Pods by that label,
not by name.

162
00:09:12,467 --> 00:09:17,630
Each Pod runs the image dlops/tic-tac-toe,
listening on port 8080.

163
00:09:17,950 --> 00:09:21,789
The Service listens
on port 80 and forwards to 8080.

164
00:09:23,033 --> 00:09:26,852
Deploy the app:
kubectl apply -f, and the file.

165
00:09:27,172 --> 00:09:29,828
Apply only hands a wish to the API server;

166
00:09:29,828 --> 00:09:34,319
the scheduler picks two nodes,
and their kubelets start the containers.

167
00:09:34,867 --> 00:09:38,152
An extra check,
rollout status, watches it happen:

168
00:09:38,152 --> 00:09:43,080
zero, then one of two available,
then rolled out, on two different workers.

169
00:09:44,817 --> 00:09:47,164
Next, kubectl get services.

170
00:09:47,484 --> 00:09:50,951
The web Service is a load balancer:

171
00:09:50,951 --> 00:09:57,489
port 80, mapped to 31366 on every node,
and on to 8080 in the Pod.

172
00:09:57,809 --> 00:09:59,921
Its external IP says pending.

173
00:10:00,467 --> 00:10:03,027
43 seconds later it still says pending.

174
00:10:03,347 --> 00:10:06,504
Which part of the cluster picture
is waiting, and on what?

175
00:10:15,350 --> 00:10:18,209
The controllers,
waiting on the cloud provider.

176
00:10:18,529 --> 00:10:22,527
On GKE they ask Google for a load balancer
and an address,

177
00:10:22,527 --> 00:10:24,801
which arrives after a short wait.

178
00:10:25,121 --> 00:10:27,681
The kind cluster has no cloud to ask.

179
00:10:28,417 --> 00:10:31,307
Pause: on GKE, wait for the external IP,

180
00:10:31,307 --> 00:10:34,774
copy it into your browser,
and the game appears.

181
00:10:36,017 --> 00:10:37,916
On the stand-in, an extra:

182
00:10:37,916 --> 00:10:41,350
port-forward to the Service, then curl:
200 OK.

183
00:10:41,670 --> 00:10:44,217
The lecture suggests it for your browser,

184
00:10:44,217 --> 00:10:48,689
but from the toolbox the port opens
inside the container: test it there.

185
00:10:49,933 --> 00:10:53,453
Back to the night-time problem:
who restarts the container?

186
00:10:53,773 --> 00:10:56,034
In Kubernetes, a controller does.

187
00:10:56,354 --> 00:10:57,784
Let's watch one work.

188
00:10:58,233 --> 00:11:00,281
An extra listing shows the chain.

189
00:11:00,601 --> 00:11:06,212
The Deployment named web owns a ReplicaSet,
whose name adds a hash of the Pod template.

190
00:11:06,532 --> 00:11:08,479
The ReplicaSet owns two Pods,

191
00:11:08,479 --> 00:11:11,566
each with that hash
and a short random suffix.

192
00:11:12,100 --> 00:11:13,491
The labels confirm it:

193
00:11:13,491 --> 00:11:17,220
every Pod carries the template hash
and the run: web label.

194
00:11:17,466 --> 00:11:20,937
The ReplicaSet controller runs a loop:
observe the Pods,

195
00:11:20,937 --> 00:11:24,655
compare with the number you declared,
act on the difference.

196
00:11:24,975 --> 00:11:26,095
You set the number;

197
00:11:26,095 --> 00:11:28,922
the controller keeps it true,
like a thermostat.

198
00:11:29,466 --> 00:11:33,413
First, one more extra:
kubectl scale --replicas=4.

199
00:11:33,733 --> 00:11:36,558
Two more Pods appear,
spread over both workers,

200
00:11:36,558 --> 00:11:38,661
and the Service already lists them.

201
00:11:38,981 --> 00:11:44,485
Scale back to 2, and remember this command:
your project's Milestone 5 asks for it.

202
00:11:45,050 --> 00:11:47,247
Now delete one of the two web Pods.

203
00:11:47,567 --> 00:11:50,039
A second later, how many web Pods are there,

204
00:11:50,039 --> 00:11:52,623
and which part of the new name
stays the same?

205
00:12:01,466 --> 00:12:02,466
Two.

206
00:12:02,533 --> 00:12:06,245
The delete returns,
and a new Pod is already being created.

207
00:12:06,565 --> 00:12:10,042
Its name keeps the ReplicaSet hash,
with a new suffix.

208
00:12:10,283 --> 00:12:12,480
15 seconds later, both run.

209
00:12:12,800 --> 00:12:15,872
Nobody logged in:
the loop noticed, and acted.

210
00:12:16,116 --> 00:12:19,068
That ticks failover,
with an honest footnote:

211
00:12:19,068 --> 00:12:21,364
it is a restart, not a hot standby.

212
00:12:21,684 --> 00:12:24,162
With one replica there is a short gap,

213
00:12:24,162 --> 00:12:27,487
which is why the game's file asks
for two replicas.

214
00:12:28,033 --> 00:12:32,193
In lecture 20, Docker restarted a container
on the same machine.

215
00:12:32,513 --> 00:12:35,990
A ReplicaSet places a new
Pod wherever there is room.

216
00:12:36,310 --> 00:12:38,700
Declare the result, don't script the steps.

217
00:12:39,950 --> 00:12:42,745
Pods are mortal:
the new Pod got a new address.

218
00:12:43,065 --> 00:12:44,814
So how does traffic find it?

219
00:12:45,134 --> 00:12:48,484
The Service keeps a list
of every Pod wearing its label,

220
00:12:48,484 --> 00:12:50,339
and that list follows the Pods.

221
00:12:52,600 --> 00:12:55,065
An extra from a throwaway Pod:

222
00:12:55,065 --> 00:13:00,078
the name web.default.svc.cluster.local
points to the Service,

223
00:13:00,078 --> 00:13:03,693
and fetching just web returns the game page.

224
00:13:04,013 --> 00:13:06,040
The name stays while the Pods change.

225
00:13:06,283 --> 00:13:10,320
This is how the cheese app's API
will find its database:

226
00:13:10,320 --> 00:13:12,555
by the Service name, vector-db.

227
00:13:12,800 --> 00:13:16,704
Another extra:
kubectl exec into the web Deployment.

228
00:13:17,024 --> 00:13:21,312
Hostname prints the name of one Pod,
and the game's files are inside.

229
00:13:21,632 --> 00:13:24,619
The README shells into the API the same way.

230
00:13:24,867 --> 00:13:26,659
Why did get all look empty?

231
00:13:26,979 --> 00:13:29,560
Namespaces: folders for objects.

232
00:13:29,880 --> 00:13:33,063
Without a flag,
kubectl looks only in default;

233
00:13:33,063 --> 00:13:38,115
this extra lists them, then creates
and deletes the cheese-app-namespace.

234
00:13:38,733 --> 00:13:43,890
So in part 2, every command
about the app gets -n cheese-app-namespace,

235
00:13:43,890 --> 00:13:45,197
in the flags slot.

236
00:13:45,833 --> 00:13:51,124
Before deleting the cluster, an extra tip:
kubectl delete with the same file.

237
00:13:51,444 --> 00:13:56,372
On GKE, this releases the load balancer
while the cluster still exists.

238
00:13:56,617 --> 00:13:58,485
Then delete the cluster:

239
00:13:58,485 --> 00:14:03,465
gcloud container clusters delete
test-cluster --zone us-east1-c.

240
00:14:03,785 --> 00:14:06,601
Yours asks whether to continue; answer yes.

241
00:14:07,150 --> 00:14:08,729
We delete the stand-in too.

242
00:14:10,650 --> 00:14:14,170
The lecture's last word fits here:
don't forget to delete.

243
00:14:14,490 --> 00:14:18,351
Pause, check that your test cluster is gone,
and part 1 is done.

